#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Combine every broker's trade book from Redis into one trades document, the way the REST API's `/api/orders/trades`
answers, and write it to the Redis key `unified:orders:trades` every half second.

    bin/unified/trades
    redis-cli GET unified:orders:trades

Nothing here calls a broker. Each broker's trade book is already kept in Redis by `bin/<broker>/trades`, as
`{"timestamp", "status", "code", "data"}` under `<broker>:orders:trades`, and the instruments by
`bin/unified/map_instruments`, so this reads those and interprets them.

**The document** has the REST API's shape:

    {"trades": [...], "summary": {"count", "buy_value", "sell_value", "total_value"}, "brokers": [...], "as_of": ...}

**A trade** is one fill, in the REST API's trade contract, the same for every broker: `broker`, `trade_id`, `order_id`,
`exchange_order_id`, `exchange_trade_id`, `instrument_id`, `instrument_token`, `tradingsymbol`, `exchange`,
`transaction_type`, `product`, `quantity`, `price`, `value`, `trade_timestamp` and `exchange_timestamp`. Side and
product are on the shared vocabulary (`BUY`/`SELL`; `CNC`, `MIS`, `NRML`...), `value` is quantity times price rounded to
paise, `exchange` stays the broker's own code, and timestamps are passed through as each broker writes them. Each
broker's fields are read exactly as the REST API's orders modules read them:

| broker | trade id, order id | token, symbol, exchange | side, product | quantity, price | times |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| zerodha | `trade_id`, `order_id` | `instrument_token`, `tradingsymbol`, `exchange` | `transaction_type`, `product` | `quantity`, `average_price` | `fill_timestamp`, `exchange_timestamp` |
| dhan | `exchangeTradeId`, `orderId` | `securityId`, `tradingSymbol`, `exchangeSegment` | `transactionType`, `productType` | `tradedQuantity`, `tradedPrice` | `createTime`, `exchangeTime` |
| flattrade, shoonya | `flid`, `norenordno` | `token`, `tsym`, `exch` | `trantype`, `prd` | `flqty`, `flprc` | `fltm`, `exch_tm` (placeholder read as none) |
| fyers | `tradeNumber`, `orderNumber` | `fyToken`, `symbol`, the symbol's prefix | `side`, `productType` | `tradedQty`, `tradePrice` | `orderDateTime` |
| groww | `groww_trade_id`, `groww_order_id` | no token, `trading_symbol`, `exchange` | `transaction_type`, `product` | `quantity`, `price` | `trade_date_time` or `created_at` |
| indmoney | `fill_id` or `trade_id`, `order_id` or `id` | `security_id`, no symbol, `exchange` | `txn_type`, `product` | `quantity` or `traded_qty`, `price` or `traded_price` | `trade_date_time` or `created_at` |
| kotak | `flId`, `nOrdNo` | `tok`, `trdSym` or `sym`, `exSeg` or `exch` | `trnsTp`, `prod` | `fldQty`, `avgPrc` | `flDt flTm`, `exTm` |
| wisdom_capital | `ExecutionID`, `AppOrderID` | `ExchangeInstrumentID`, `TradingSymbol`, `ExchangeSegment` | `OrderSide`, `ProductType` | `LastTradedQuantity`, `LastTradedPrice` | `LastExecutionTransactTime`, `ExchangeTransactTime` |

**Resolving** follows the REST API. A broker's exchange code names an exchange and a kind of instrument - cash,
derivative, currency or commodity - and a token is only unique within one of an exchange's scrip files, so a token is
looked up in `unified:broker_tokens` and kept only among the segments its venue's kind trades on that exchange; several
survivors are taken in vocabulary order, then by symbol and id. Groww sends no token, so a Groww cash trade is looked up by
its symbol in `unified:instrument_symbols`. A trade nothing resolves has a null `instrument_id`; a resolved trade the
broker gave no symbol takes the instrument's. Resolutions are remembered until the cache's mapping date changes.

**Not merged, and sorted** by broker, then trade time, then trade id, since each broker writes times its own way.
`summary` adds `value` by side. `brokers` says, for each broker, how its stored book was read: `ok`, `stale` (stored more
than a minute ago - its script may have stopped; its trades are still included), `missing` (no key) or `unreadable`,
with `as_of`, when its script stored it.

Written with SET every half second. Runs until Ctrl+C or SIGTERM; a Redis that cannot be reached is logged and tried
again. Reads only - it places no orders. Stoxkart is not included: it has no bin scripts.
"""

